        },
        "markdown": "# 2 · Simulated Breaking-News Future\n## 3:14–6:09\n\nLionel asks viewers to follow him through an imagined future news event. He performs the breaking-news language, predicts a conventional explanation for the death, imagines commentators repeating it, and then shows how each unresolved question would be declared moot. \n\n**Function:** make an abstract risk vivid by temporarily moving the audience into a hypothetical future.\n\nThis is the strongest new rhetorical move in the specimen:\n\n## `Lionel Runs a Future Scenario`\n\nIt probably does not require a new composition template.",
        "memo": "",
        "link": "",
        "visibilityRole": "browser",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "create",
          "updatedAt": 1786060285808,
          "reason": "node-batch-create"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "lionel-study07-seg3",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "3 · Historical Precedent for Narrative Closure",
