        },
        "markdown": "# 3 · Historical Precedent for Narrative Closure\n## 6:09–10:16\n\nLionel argues that covertly caused deaths can be difficult to recognize and then presents Jeffrey Epstein as his principal historical analogy. He focuses less on operational mechanism than on his claim that authorities and the public accepted a suicide conclusion and rapidly moved on. \n\n**Function:** support the plausibility of the projected future by supplying an earlier event that Lionel believes follows the same pattern.\n\n**Existing moves:** `Lionel Adds Historical Context`, `Lionel Adds Analogy`, `Lionel Identifies Pattern`.",
        "memo": "",
        "link": "",
        "visibilityRole": "browser",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "create",
          "updatedAt": 1786060285808,
          "reason": "node-batch-create"
        }
      }
    },
